					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3938622&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607546656" alt="The Great Hippo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3938622)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/the-great-hippo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3938622); return false;" >The Great Hippo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1603292122 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">21 Oct 2020 14:55</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4801040">
									

<p>This already did <em>way</em> better than I expected it to, so I figured I'd explain a couple of things for anyone curious:</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">AUTHOR'S&nbsp;NOTES</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<ul>
<li>The story is semi-real (based off an actual I-beam that held up a line when it had every reason to fail). The only real difference are the stakes &#8212; if the I-beam had fallen, it would have killed everyone on the unit (not everyone in the city).</li>
<li>Hydrofluoric alkylation units are very real, and they are very terrifying.</li>
<li>The only reason Jefferson Reeds says hydrofluoric acid wouldn't make number 1 in 'Worst Chemicals to Work With' is because things like <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dimethylmercury">dimethylmercury</a> exist. If you're curious to read more about horrifying chemistry, here's a great <a href="https://blogs.sciencemag.org/pipeline/archives/2008/02/26/sand_wont_save_you_this_time">blog</a> on that very subject (thanks to <span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jekeled" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1349098); return false;"><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1349098&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1603293069" alt="Jekeled" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1349098)" /></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/jekeled" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1349098); return false;">Jekeled</a></span> for reminding me that it exists).</li>
<li>This is also based off the explosion in the Philadelphia Refinery in 2019 &#8212; which, yes, happened in an HF unit &#8212; and yes, they had an emergency shutdown system (which, thank God, worked precisely as intended).</li>
<li>In the story, Daniel Ruhl refuses to open the hydrant-line because ice on a hydrant in July means something is <em>very</em> wrong. He just hadn't figured out what, yet.</li>
<li>As for backing liquid propane into your fire-suppression system&#8230; yes, it can happen (and yes, it <em>has</em> happened). Ice on your hydrants is one of the big warning signs.</li>
<li>It's cheesy as fuck, but I wrote this story in a fugue state after hearing <em>We All Lift Together</em> &#8212; specifically, the line: <em>Feel the weight of what we owe</em>. When you build a refinery in the heart of a city, you're putting the people of that city directly in the line of fire. Something is owed. The stakeholders, owners, and supervisors might not feel the weight of that debt &#8212; but <em>someone</em> has to.</li>
<li>When Tanya Sullivan says 'we'd be damned if we let this place take anyone else with it', she's thinking literally: the steel beam didn't just save the city; it spared the workers the guilt of having killed an entire city's worth of people. In her mind, and in the mind of Matthew Watkins, that beam saved their souls.</li>
<li>Ultimately, the only real anomaly here&#8230; is that this refinery <strong>did not</strong> kill the city.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
